### Servlet — `RegisterServlet.java`

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.sql.*;
import javax.servlet.*;
import javax.servlet.http.*;
import javax.servlet.annotation.WebServlet;

@WebServlet("/RegisterServlet")
public class RegisterServlet extends HttpServlet {
    protected void doPost(HttpServletRequest req,
        HttpServletResponse res) throws IOException {

        res.setContentType("text/html");
        PrintWriter out = res.getWriter();

        try {
            Connection con = DriverManager.getConnection(
                "jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/webdb",
                "root", "password");

            PreparedStatement ps = con.prepareStatement(
                "INSERT INTO users VALUES(?,?,?,?)");

            ps.setString(1, req.getParameter("name"));
            ps.setString(2, req.getParameter("password"));
            ps.setString(3, req.getParameter("email"));
            ps.setString(4, req.getParameter("country"));

            ps.executeUpdate();
            out.println("Registration successful");

            con.close();
        } catch (Exception e) {
            out.println("Error: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
```

For a real application, passwords must be hashed before storage. This simplified version illustrates JDBC registration for a lab exercise.

